WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.160
When we first released Claude Code,

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:03.520
it was like a little video

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:04.880
and I remember posting it to Slack,

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.600
and there was like two people
that gave like the reactions

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.360
and like people were like excited.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:10.800
I thought it was really cool,

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.680
especially for my very easy
engineering tasks.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:14.640
It was quite good at it.

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.000
That's like a really nice way
to say that

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:29.520
it wasn't really good.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:32.720
I can't believe
it's only been a year

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:34.480
since we first launched
Claude Code.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:36.960
It's hard to remember what
what that was like.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.720
Like, it’s so different
than what we're doing today.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:42.800
Like, now I just have, like,
armies of agents

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:44.560
that are doing stuff like I'm

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:46.560
prompting one agent
or I have like an agent

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:48.560
that's like prompting agents,
that's prompting agents.

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:50.880
And it's like a tree
of like thousands of agents.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.040
But I think it's just like
the most important idea

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:54.000
when working on this stuff

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.200
is like, every single time
Claude makes a mistake.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:58.960
I don't tell Claude
to do it differently,

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:00.880
I tell it to write it
to the CLAUDE.md,

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:02.480
or to like make a skill or

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.480
or something
to do it differently.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.000
And if you can do this,
then Claude

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.600
can just like run forever.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:09.680
And I think the other thing
that we kind of realize is

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:11.600
the verification
is really important.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:12.640
Like we didn't realize that.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.200
I hear this come up
a lot with developers

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:17.520
and enterprises
that we meet with.

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:19.680
What are your tips for making
a really good

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.760
making Claude Code really good
at verification?

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:22.720
I sort of feel like this

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:23.840
is this thing that

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:25.280
just like everyone misunderstands

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:27.360
because whenever we talk about
verification,

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:28.720
people are thinking like unit tests

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:31.280
or they're thinking like lint
or like type check.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:32.400
These are the things that are

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:33.920
obviously really easy to automate.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.480
And these are the things
that were already automated.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.000
But actually when we talk
about verification

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.160
for agents, it's
something slightly different.

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:42.240
It's like can the agent run
the thing?

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.080
It takes a little bit of
mental work

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:45.920
to figure out
how exactly do you do this,

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:47.600
because it's often
not straightforward.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:48.560
And I think that's like,

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.400
that’s one of the challenges.
I remember

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.720
I remember with Opus 4

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:54.720
Claude tested itself.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:58.000
And we just like
hooked it up to Opus 4

00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.840
And I was like, Claude
build the feature

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:02.560
and then test yourself
in like bash.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.920
And it opened a little Claude
CLI and tested

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:07.520
its own feature.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:10.000
And I was just like, whoa.

00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:10.720
It's crazy!

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:12.160
Like now,
now we're so used to it.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:14.640
Like now, you know, now
we have these loops going for,

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:15.760
you know,
like the iOS simulator

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.160
and the Android simulator
and like computers

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:20.480
for desktop,
like it's not surprising.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:22.080
But back then that was crazy.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:24.480
How are, like,
how are you doing it?

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:25.920
So I've been mainly

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.920
hacking on the desktop app
these days.

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:29.760
And one of the engineers
on the team

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:31.040
actually added this desktop

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:32.000
development skill

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:35.040
that teaches Claude how
to run the local desktop app.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:36.480
And I've been having it
use it,

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.160
and it still runs into issues
or like bugs

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.320
with the staging environment
sometimes.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:44.400
And so what I have it do
is in those cases,

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.640
I have it read
Slack and understand, hey,

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:48.320
is staging down right now?

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:51.680
Or has someone
else already hit this?

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:52.800
And then when it

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.160
debugs the whole issue,

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:56.320
I tell it to update
the desktop development skill.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:57.840
What the skill does is

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:00.160
Claude actually spins up
a local desktop app,

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:03.120
and it uses computer
use to click around on it.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.200
And so when I add a new UX,

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:07.920
it clicks around
to invoke the new UX.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:09.840
It also tests edge cases,

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:13.040
and when there's an issue
it fixes it and re-checks.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:14.880
This is like honestly,
one of my favorite things

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.920
about this team
is everyone codes.

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:21.360
I've never been on a
team where, like,

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:23.360
my PM would code

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:25.920
and it's like crazy and like
your code is like really good.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:27.840
You’re too nice.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:30.480
But I also just feel like it's

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:31.840
it's also just becoming easier

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:34.720
because it's like essentially
Claude writes the code.

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:36.720
And so what matters
a little more is like,

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:38.480
what's the idea that you have?

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:39.520
And I feel like

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:40.080
if you're a person

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:41.120
that has like
the product context

00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:42.480
and the business context
and you're thinking

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.160
about the design and the user,

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:46.160
you're just going to come up
with better ideas.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:48.160
It's kind of like
all the roles are merging.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.800
I remember seeing Megan
our designer’s PRs

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:52.400
and I was just horrified
at the beginning

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:54.800
I was like, oh my God, why is
Megan putting up PRs?

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:56.000
And then she was like,
yeah, yeah.

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:57.760
I'm just like,
I'm fixing the button.

00:03:57.760 --> 00:03:58.480
And I was like,

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:00.240
okay, all right, well,
the code looks good,

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.160
so maybe it's maybe it's fine.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:04.560
And I feel like now it's
just like it's totally normal.

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:05.760
Yeah, and we see this across

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.680
all the enterprises we talk with.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:10.880
Like, it's the engineers adopt
Claude Code first

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:14.720
and then the, the eng adjecent
roles look over their shoulder

00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:16.800
and they're like, whoa,
this thing is very powerful.

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:19.680
Let me try it out.
And we found it's crazy.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:20.560
We found that, like,

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:23.280
our designers are more
productive making prototypes

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:25.120
and making changes
directly in the app

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:26.800
instead of pinging an engineer,

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:29.680
PMs are making changes in the app.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:32.160
Our finance team runs
and in Claude Code,

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:34.320
they do their projections there.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:35.680
Data science.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.800
Like if you talk with our
data scientists, it's so cool.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:40.480
It's just like everyone just has

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:42.240
Claude Codes up on their screens.

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:46.160
I feel like it's remarkably versatile

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:47.200
for different roles.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:48.560
What do you feel like nowadays,

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:51.600
are the use cases
that are pushing the limit?

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:54.800
One that I'm super excited
about is routines.

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:56.640
There is one engineer
on our team

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:59.840
who launched voice mode across
all of our products.

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:03.920
And, he has his routine set up
that just listens for

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:07.120
every ticket that comes,
every GitHub issue, every

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:08.800
bug report about voice mode.

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:10.640
And his Claude just picks it up,

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:14.560
proactively puts up a fix,
and then pings the PR to him.

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:15.840
And when he got that working

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:17.280
for voice, he thought, okay,

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:18.080
we're getting a lot

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:19.280
of other feedback

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:21.280
that isn't being responded to.

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:24.240
So, he also set up a routine
to listen for that.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:26.640
So I ship this, small feature.

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:29.280
And there was like an edge
case in it that I didn't see.

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:31.360
And so someone filed
a bug for it,

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:34.640
and I was going to
get to the bug that night.

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:37.200
And my Claude was working,
it said, wait a second,

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:39.360
another Claude
has already fixed this.

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:40.800
And I was like,
how is this possible?

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:41.840
Like, I've never talked to him

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:43.040
about this feature before

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:43.760
and so I pinged him,

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:45.360
and I was like,
how did you fix this

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:46.080
so quickly?

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:47.600
And he said,
he has another routine

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:49.200
that just looks
for bug reports

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:51.440
that haven't been responded
to in five hours

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:53.840
and puts up a fix,
and he merges the ones that

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:55.840
are easy to verify.

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:57.760
Claude tells me this
like all the time now.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:05:59.280
That someone else
has already fixed it?

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:00.400
There's always like
another person’s

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:01.840
Claude that's working on it.

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:04.400
It's like, yeah, that's
been one of the changes.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:05.440
I feel like we're,

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:07.440
a while ago
we were trying to figure out,

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:08.400
like, how to use routines,

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:10.240
and I feel like
just like the agent SDK

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:14.320
was this first idea
that we could use Claude Code

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:15.680
programmatically. But

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:18.160
I feel like at the beginning,
it just wasn't obvious.

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:20.320
How do we use it?
What do we use it for?

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:21.920
And I think routines are

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:24.400
the first
really obvious application.

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:26.320
And I don't know, like

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:28.240
it just does like all the code
review,

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:30.720
it babysits like every PR,

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:32.880
you remember back in the day
you used to actually

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:34.880
have to like respond to code
review comments.

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:38.560
You used to have to like
fix CI. You used to have to rebase.

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:40.960
Yeah. Like I haven't done that
in a long time.

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:41.360
Yeah.

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:42.880
When you're in the CLI

00:06:42.880 --> 00:06:45.120
and you're synchronously
working with Claude,

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:47.280
what are your go to features?

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:47.600
Okay.

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:49.840
What they used to
be is plan mode.

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:51.360
I don't use that anymore.

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:52.400
What do you use instead?

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:53.200
Auto mode.

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:53.840
Auto mode?

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:54.480
It’s the best.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:55.600
Instead of plan mode?

00:06:55.600 --> 00:06:56.480
Instead of plan mode.

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:58.240
Yeah because the newer models

00:06:58.240 --> 00:07:01.120
they don't actually need
like a planning step anymore.

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:02.160
I think this was really

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:05.520
important for like Opus 4
through maybe 4.5.

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:07.440
Then I think starting with
four six and definitely with

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:09.520
four seven, it just doesn't
need that planning step.

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:10.720
I think some people
still use it.

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:11.840
They like to have that artifact.

00:07:11.840 --> 00:07:13.120
I don't use it

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:14.720
And I just do
auto mode for everything

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:16.640
because then I start my Claude,

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:17.440
it starts to work

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:19.520
and then I just like move on
to the next Claude

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:21.120
and I don't have to sit there
and watch it.

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:22.800
But from the very early
stage we had this

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:25.840
like permission prompts model
for Claude Code, right?

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:27.520
Like it runs a tool
and then it asks you like,

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:29.520
hey, are you okay
running this tool?

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:31.680
And you had to say yes or no.

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:32.880
And at the time,

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:34.880
that was kind of the best
we had a year and a half ago

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:36.720
because we didn't have,
you know, classifiers.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:38.880
The model was not as well
aligned as it is today.

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:40.320
So auto mode was just such a

00:07:40.320 --> 00:07:42.560
it was such a big step up
because actually

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:44.560
you don't want to read
most of these requests.

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:46.080
Just routing it
to a different model

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:48.720
and having it check for
security works so much better.

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:49.280
Yeah.

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:51.760
And if a thing like
is a little suss or,

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:53.680
you know,
this isn't the command that

00:07:53.680 --> 00:07:56.400
you think you want to run
or it's not safe,

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:57.760
the model will just deny it.

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:00.160
And then you can go back
and you can allow it later.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:02.480
I think this has been
one of those, like, step changes.

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:03.440
We just, there's no way

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:04.960
we could have done this a year
and a half ago.

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:05.920
It's just human nature,

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:09.760
when you accept
99% of requests, that your eyes

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:11.520
just glaze over
when you read it.

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:14.640
And so actually, we feel
that auto mode is more safe

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:15.280
than reading

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:16.800
every single
permission prompt,

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:17.520
because it means

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:20.640
that your only paying attention
to the most important thing

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:22.320
and not like being spammed

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:24.720
a bunch of things
that are just 99% yes.

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:26.240
I think security
is one of these things.

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:27.280
Like you can talk about it

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:28.080
and then

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:30.560
it's a totally different thing
to actually do it correctly,

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:32.160
because it just doesn't
always look

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:33.440
the way that you think
it's going to look.

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:34.480
And it's just all about

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:37.600
like always red teaming,
always pentesting

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:38.480
always looking,

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:39.760
you know,
always having a threat model

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:41.280
and then using that
to figure out,

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:43.280
you know, how is this thing
going to get attacked?

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:45.440
How are people going to get
prompt injected?

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:47.200
And I just feel like
like the team

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:48.800
is just like obsessed
with this.

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:52.000
And it's so important
because as a result,

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:53.840
I just trust the agent to run

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:55.280
and I can move on

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:57.520
and I can just have
like a second agent.

00:08:57.520 --> 00:08:58.720
And if I didn't trust it,

00:08:58.720 --> 00:09:00.960
then I just wouldn't
have been able to do that.

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:02.400
And internally,

00:09:02.400 --> 00:09:05.200
to actually get auto
mode out to our users,

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:07.520
we needed to really
trust it first.

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:10.480
And so what we did
was we collected thousands

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:14.000
of transcripts
of like an entire agent

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:15.920
trajectory
and a permission prompt

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:18.960
and had auto mode classify
whether or not it was safe.

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:20.480
And it was extremely good
at this.

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:21.920
So then we got red teamers,

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:22.880
and we asked them

00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:25.920
to try to prompt inject,
and try to hack

00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:27.120
the code base.

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:28.960
And we use this
to create evals

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:30.800
and make sure
that all of these were denied.

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:33.200
And then we had our own
internal teams

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:37.440
try to prompt inject and
hack Claude Code’s auto mode.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:39.120
And then we improved auto mode

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:40.720
to make sure
that we caught all of these.

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:42.400
So it's not only just protecting you

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:43.840
against the vulnerabilities

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:45.840
that are out there
in the wild today, but,

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:50.240
the most intelligent attacks
that we can construct.

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:51.040
Yeah. I mean, it's like,

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:52.720
it’s honestly
like a weird approach.

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:54.720
I feel like there's, like,
all these features

00:09:54.720 --> 00:09:55.440
the last year

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:57.040
where the first time
someone pitched it,

00:09:57.040 --> 00:09:59.360
I was like, no way,
that's not going to work.

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:00.720
And I feel like over time
I just learned,

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:03.440
like I'm actually wrong,
like so often now.

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:06.560
Because, like, building on
the model is so weird.

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:07.520
It's just like all this,

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:08.560
like, engineering stuff

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:09.920
that I've learned over the
years.

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:11.680
So much of it I just have to, like,
throw out.

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:13.520
And this is just like part
of what the job is now.

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:14.400
We're building on a new thing

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:16.560
and we just have
to relearn it.

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:18.560
And auto mode was
definitely one of these.

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:19.760
I was like,
the first time I heard it,

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:21.680
I was like, route
the prompt for a model?

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:23.040
No way.
That's not going to work.

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:24.880
And then it actually
turns out empirically,

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:26.120
it works really, really well.

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:28.240
But I heard you also love
loop.

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:30.160
Yeah, I love loop.

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:31.200
How do you use it?

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:32.720
I think for loop, there's

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:34.480
this transition
that we went through

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:36.240
like a year and a half ago

00:10:36.240 --> 00:10:39.440
where we were like,
all right there’s source code.

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:42.800
But actually the thing
an engineer should interact

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:44.320
with, maybe
it's not the source code,

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:45.680
maybe it's the agent.

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:47.040
And so we made this leap of

00:10:47.600 --> 00:10:48.800
I don't write the source code,

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:50.240
I talked to an agent,
and the agent writes

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:51.440
the source code for me.

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:53.040
And I think right now
what's happening

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:55.040
is we're making the next leap.

00:10:55.040 --> 00:10:56.720
I don't talk to an agent
anymore.

00:10:56.720 --> 00:10:59.440
I talk to loop or I talk to a routine

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:02.080
and it prompts Claude for me.

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:04.400
And it's just it's crazy.

00:11:04.400 --> 00:11:05.840
I mean, it's been like,
it's a year and a half

00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:07.360
and this was like
two big leaps.

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:09.680
If you take like, a step back,
how are you

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:12.400
seeing entire engineering
orgs change?

00:11:12.400 --> 00:11:14.640
I'm going to put on my
business cat hat.

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:16.400
I have this, like,
favorite case study.

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:18.800
This is like a Harvard
Business Review from the 90s.

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:20.880
And they were talking about,
like, computers are here.

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:23.120
Why are we not seeing
the productivity benefits?

00:11:23.120 --> 00:11:25.760
And it's just this, like
amazing snapshot into like,

00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:27.600
what it actually felt like
at the time

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:29.680
because, like, you know,
people used to use mainframes.

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:32.640
At some point companies
switch to personal computers.

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:34.240
It was sort of a new thing,
and the companies were trying

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:34.960
to figure out

00:11:34.960 --> 00:11:35.840
how to use it.

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:37.040
The same way
they're trying to figure out

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:38.800
how to use AI right now.

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:41.360
And it turned out that
to get the productivity

00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:44.240
benefits from computers,
what you had to do isn't like

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:45.120
you have your paper

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:47.680
filing cabinet and your, like,
paper and pen

00:11:47.680 --> 00:11:48.720
business process.

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:49.440
And then there's like

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:51.840
a computer on the side
that does something.

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:52.800
Actually, what you have to do

00:11:52.800 --> 00:11:54.320
is you throw out the filing
cabinet,

00:11:54.320 --> 00:11:56.480
you have to throw out all
your paper and all your pens,

00:11:56.480 --> 00:11:58.160
and then you put a computer
in the center

00:11:58.160 --> 00:11:59.920
and everything has to run
through the computer.

00:11:59.920 --> 00:12:01.920
It has to be at the center
of every business process.

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:03.440
And I feel like at Anthropic

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:05.840
we do this thing
where when you on board,

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:08.800
you don't ask people questions
like no one asks me

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:09.840
questions when they on board.

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:12.960
You probably have
the same thing, they ask Claude.

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:14.720
And this
is kind of weird, like,

00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:17.040
this is the first company
I've been at like that.

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:18.320
And I feel like for us, Claude

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:19.360
is just at the center
of everything.

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:21.360
Whenever I have a question,
I ask Claude.

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:22.960
Whenever I write code,
I use Claude.

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:25.520
Whenever I need a code
review, Claude does it,

00:12:25.520 --> 00:12:27.840
whenever I need a security
review, Claude does it,

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:28.640
whenever I need to

00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:30.080
you know,
fill out a form or something,

00:12:30.080 --> 00:12:31.200
Co-work does it.

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:33.760
So it's just like Claude is
at the center of everything.

00:12:33.760 --> 00:12:35.200
And I feel like the companies

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:36.400
that are really
figuring it out,

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:38.240
and there's
a bunch of them now,

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:40.080
they're just putting
Claude at the center of it.

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:41.200
And I think for computers,

00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:43.600
the transition
took 10 to 15 years.

00:12:43.600 --> 00:12:46.080
But actually for AI,
because so much of our work

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:49.840
is already digitized
and Claude can use a computer

00:12:49.840 --> 00:12:52.000
and it can write code
and run code.

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:54.000
This transition is happening
a lot faster.

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:55.680
I think it's just like,
really, it's

00:12:55.680 --> 00:12:56.720
just really exciting.

00:12:56.720 --> 00:12:57.600
Like,

00:12:57.600 --> 00:12:58.240
I feel like

00:12:58.240 --> 00:13:00.400
now I don't
have to bug people anymore

00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:01.040
and when I

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:02.080
interact with people, it's

00:13:02.080 --> 00:13:02.960
because it's like fun

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:04.640
and I get to collaborate
with them on stuff

00:13:04.640 --> 00:13:06.640
and we get to create something
together.

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:08.000
It's not that like,
I need them.

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:09.600
I need something,

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:10.560
you know, from them

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:11.920
because, like, Claude
can actually do

00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:13.200
a lot of that stuff now.

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:14.720
And I also feel like
as an engineer,

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:15.920
I've just never had this much

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:17.680
fun doing engineering
because the

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:19.200
like the tedious part
I don't have to do.

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:20.480
Like I'm
just coming up with ideas.

00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:24.400
I'm talking to customers
and every idea, like,

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:25.680
I don't have a
to do list anymore.

00:13:25.680 --> 00:13:27.520
Like Claude
just builds everything.

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:28.640
And so my job is to come up

00:13:28.640 --> 00:13:30.560
with these ideas
and it's just so fun.

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:31.600
Okay, so here's a question.

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:33.600
Is the future product or engineering?

00:13:33.600 --> 00:13:34.720
Like, is everyone going to be a PM

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:36.400
or is everyone going to be
an engineer?

00:13:36.400 --> 00:13:38.400
Everyone's going to be both.

00:13:38.400 --> 00:13:41.600
I feel pretty strongly
that these roles are merging.

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:44.720
Like when we look at our team,
our product team

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:45.840
all writes code.

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:50.000
Our Devrel team all writes code.
Our design team all writes code.

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:50.960
And then we look at our

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:54.560
engineers and a lot of them
ship products end to end.

00:13:54.560 --> 00:13:56.640
They have an idea for what
to build.

00:13:56.640 --> 00:13:57.920
They build it.

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:00.400
They work with legal
and marketing to figure out

00:14:00.400 --> 00:14:01.920
how we communicate this
to the world

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:04.480
and make sure it's safe
and with security, too.

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:06.160
And a lot of times
they just see through

00:14:06.160 --> 00:14:08.000
this whole process end to end.

00:14:08.000 --> 00:14:11.280
So I think right now
AI really benefits people

00:14:11.280 --> 00:14:14.720
people who have a lot of curiosity,
have a lot of product tastes

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:18.240
who love to have this
like end to end ownership.

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:19.680
And now a lot of people

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:22.000
are running
like hundreds of agents.

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:23.680
What are the products
that you think

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:24.800
people should be adopting

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:29.120
as they transition from single
to multiple to hundreds?

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:30.240
Until recently,

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:34.160
the way that I wrote code was
I had like six terminal tabs

00:14:34.160 --> 00:14:36.480
with six git checkout
on the same repo,

00:14:36.480 --> 00:14:39.040
and then I would just like tab
between them.

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:40.080
Now it's pretty different.

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:41.360
I have like one tab,

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:43.360
I use the new agent view
that we just shipped.

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:44.640
It's like so good.

00:14:44.640 --> 00:14:46.160
And I'm so glad
that we took a while

00:14:46.160 --> 00:14:48.560
to iterate on it
to make that really good.

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:50.880
And I also use the desktop app

00:14:50.880 --> 00:14:53.280
because I don't have to fiddle
with checkouts that way.

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:54.160
It just like, you know,

00:14:54.160 --> 00:14:56.640
it does the work tree cloning
or the like, it

00:14:56.640 --> 00:14:58.640
creates the work trees for me.

00:14:58.640 --> 00:15:00.720
And the thing
that I would not have expected

00:15:00.720 --> 00:15:03.920
six months ago is probably
half my engineering now

00:15:03.920 --> 00:15:05.600
I do on my phone.

00:15:05.600 --> 00:15:07.840
So I just have like
I have so many agents

00:15:07.840 --> 00:15:09.440
running that
I just start for my phone.

00:15:09.440 --> 00:15:12.240
I use a remote control,
which is like amazing now

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:14.080
and like I will start
something on my computer.

00:15:14.080 --> 00:15:16.160
And then I’ll just remote
control in from my phone

00:15:16.160 --> 00:15:17.280
and I’ll just like, walk around

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:18.160
I’ll get coffee,

00:15:18.160 --> 00:15:19.920
and then I'll check in
on my agents

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:21.840
and maybe
I'll start another agent.

00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:23.520
And sometimes I'm like,
talking to someone

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:25.200
and we come up
with a new idea.

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:26.720
I’ll just start an agent on the spot.

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:28.480
I like talk to it
with voice mode

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:30.000
and just have it build
something,

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:32.240
and I don't even have to go
back to my computer anymore.

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:33.920
I remember
when you started doing this

00:15:33.920 --> 00:15:35.600
because you would actually

00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:36.320
leave work,

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:37.920
have your computer
on your desk

00:15:37.920 --> 00:15:40.320
open, plugged
in, screen locked,

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:42.400
and I just thought you would,
like, come back to the office

00:15:42.400 --> 00:15:44.000
at some point
to get your computer,

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:45.920
but then it would be like
pretty late and I was like,

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:48.800
maybe he just left it here by accident.

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:50.480
And then it happened again
the next day.

00:15:50.480 --> 00:15:52.000
And then it happened again
the next day.

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:52.800
And I was like,

00:15:52.800 --> 00:15:54.960
wait, it's so weird
because you're landing PRs

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:57.200
but your computer is right
next to me,

00:15:57.200 --> 00:15:59.760
and I remember you responding
and you're like, yeah,

00:15:59.760 --> 00:16:01.280
I'm coding from my couch.

00:16:01.280 --> 00:16:02.240
Yeah,

00:16:02.240 --> 00:16:04.400
that was the week the remote
control got really good.

00:16:04.400 --> 00:16:04.960
Yeah.

00:16:04.960 --> 00:16:06.560
So another thing that users

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:08.480
are asking
about all the time is

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:10.160
how do you do
context engineering,

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:12.080
especially
in a large enterprise?

00:16:12.080 --> 00:16:12.560
This is a thing.

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:14.320
You know, people used to talk
about prompt engineering.

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:16.480
They used to like work
context engineering.

00:16:16.480 --> 00:16:20.880
This is sort of matching where
the model was at the time.

00:16:20.880 --> 00:16:22.480
Back in the days
of Sonnet 3.5,

00:16:22.480 --> 00:16:25.200
you had to prompt engineer
back in the days of Opus 4,

00:16:25.200 --> 00:16:26.720
you had to context engineer.

00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:29.520
But with the models of today,
you don't do any of this.

00:16:29.520 --> 00:16:32.160
You give it the minimal
possible system prompt,

00:16:32.160 --> 00:16:34.000
the minimal possible tools,

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:36.240
and then you let the model
figure it out.

00:16:36.240 --> 00:16:37.040
Like you just have to give

00:16:37.040 --> 00:16:38.560
the model some way
to pull in the context.

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:40.400
I think that's
the most important thing.

00:16:40.400 --> 00:16:41.440
How do you think about it?

00:16:41.440 --> 00:16:42.720
I see things very similarly.

00:16:42.720 --> 00:16:46.880
I'm a context minimalist,
so my general philosophy is

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:50.080
tell the model
only what it needs to know

00:16:50.080 --> 00:16:53.120
and let it figure out
the rest of it.

00:16:53.120 --> 00:16:54.400
I think
when you give the model

00:16:54.400 --> 00:16:56.240
too much context, it's
kind of like

00:16:56.240 --> 00:16:58.080
you're micromanaging it.

00:16:58.080 --> 00:17:00.640
And sometimes the model knows
a better way

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:02.480
to get to the same outcome.

00:17:02.480 --> 00:17:05.600
And I personally prefer
to give the model that freedom

00:17:05.600 --> 00:17:07.280
to do that.

00:17:07.280 --> 00:17:08.400
And then in general, we're

00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:10.240
also making our harness
more lean

00:17:10.240 --> 00:17:13.280
so that you have more room
for your own prompts.

00:17:13.280 --> 00:17:14.960
And so that follows
your prompts better.

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:17.280
There's all these
different ways to Claude now,

00:17:17.280 --> 00:17:18.720
but I feel like in a year it's

00:17:18.720 --> 00:17:20.880
going to be
a totally new set of things,

00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:22.880
and it's
going to be so surprising

00:17:22.880 --> 00:17:24.400
if it's still these
same things,

00:17:24.400 --> 00:17:25.440
because I think, like

00:17:25.440 --> 00:17:27.520
we're seeing
these giant trends happening

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:29.360
right now,
agents are running for longer.

00:17:29.360 --> 00:17:31.040
They're more autonomous.

00:17:31.040 --> 00:17:33.760
Very rarely am I running one
agent at a time.

00:17:33.760 --> 00:17:35.360
It's usually like a few agents

00:17:35.360 --> 00:17:37.440
or dozens
or hundreds or thousands.

00:17:37.440 --> 00:17:39.520
And so like the form factor
for that, it's

00:17:39.520 --> 00:17:41.520
going to be really different
than what came before.

00:17:41.520 --> 00:17:42.800
And I don't know
what it's going to be.

00:17:42.800 --> 00:17:44.480
And I think
in a large part, it's

00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:46.560
going to be up to the team
to figure it out.

00:17:46.560 --> 00:17:47.680
And this is,

00:17:47.680 --> 00:17:48.400
this is why I'm like,

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:51.040
so happy we run the team
that the way that we do,

00:17:51.040 --> 00:17:52.560
where everyone just comes up
with ideas

00:17:52.560 --> 00:17:55.200
and everyone is able
to think about the product.

00:17:55.200 --> 00:17:57.200
Everyone talks
to users all the time

00:17:57.200 --> 00:17:58.400
because I don't think
these ideas

00:17:58.400 --> 00:17:59.440
are going to come from us.

00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:00.720
It's going to come
from the team.

00:18:00.720 --> 00:18:02.080
Totally, and from everyone

00:18:02.080 --> 00:18:05.440
in our community
building with us.

